            <ArticleTitle>Magnetic resonance imaging of epiploic appendagitis in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2123-2125</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0022-3468(16)30404-3</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpedsurg.2016.09.052</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In children, epiploic appendagitis has been seldom reported. We describe two children with clinical presentations mimicking appendicitis. A correct diagnosis was eventually achieved by magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and confirmed at laparoscopy in the initial case. Our preliminary experience suggests that MRI is a valid and non-invasive alternative to computed tomography for characterization of unusual causes of pediatric abdominal pain in the acute hospital care setting.</AbstractText>
